{"id":"51c63d1a-d946-4b99-89f0-42291c22e019","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022467,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters = case['clusters']\\n    parent_map = case['parent']\\n\\n    # Build original cluster info\\n    orig = {c['cluster_id']: c for c in clusters}\\n\\n    # Compute retained clusters with stability\\n    retained_info = {}\\n    for c in clusters:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        size = c['size']\\n        if size >= min_size:\\n            stability = sum(max(0, lam - c['birth_lambda']) for lam in c['point_exit_lambdas'])\\n            retained_info[cid] = {'size': size, 'stability': stability}\\n\\n    # Build condensed parent mapping (skip non-retained)\\n    condensed_parent = {}\\n    for cid in retained_info:\\n        cur = cid\\n        while True:\\n            p = parent_map.get(cur)\\n            if p is None:\\n                condensed_parent[cid] = None\\n                break\\n            if p in retained_info:\\n                condensed_parent[cid] = p\\n                break\\n            cur = p\\n\\n    # Build tree structure for retained clusters\\n    tree = {cid: {'parent': condensed_parent[cid], 'children': []} for cid in retained_info}\\n    for cid in retained_info:\\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\\n        if p is not None:\\n            tree[p]['children'].append(cid)\\n\\n    # Compute descendants for each cluster\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def get_descendants(cid):\\n        if cid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[cid]\\n        res = set()\\n        for child in tree[cid]['children']:\\n            res.add(child)\\n            res.update(get_descendants(child))\\n        descendants[cid] = res\\n        return res\\n    for cid in retained_info:\\n        get_descendants(cid)\\n\\n    # Sort by decreasing stability, then cluster_id ascending\\n    sorted_cids = sorted(retained_info.keys(), key=lambda cid: (-retained_info[cid]['stability'], cid))\\n\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for cid in sorted_cids:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        if descendants[cid] & selected:\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        # Block all ancestors\\n        cur = condensed_parent[cid]\\n        while cur is not None:\\n            blocked.add(cur)\\n            cur = condensed_parent[cur]\\n\\n    # Build output\\n    out_clusters = []\\n    for c in clusters:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        if cid in retained_info:\\n            out_clusters.append({\\n                'cluster_id': cid,\\n                'size': retained_info[cid]['size'],\\n                'stability': retained_info[cid]['stability']\\n            })\\n    selected_ids = sorted(selected)\\n    return {'clusters': out_clusters, 'selected_cluster_ids': selected_ids}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task DATA-HDB-02. The input payload has fields: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of dicts with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent (dict mapping child cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null for root). Also _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nWe must produce output with \"clusters\" array and \"selected_cluster_ids\" array.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Validate input: check min_cluster_size is integer >=0? Actually min_cluster_size is given, we need to drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n- For each cluster, compute stability: sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda)) over its point_exit_lambdas. Note: birth_lambda is per cluster, point_exit_lambdas list per cluster.\n- Retain clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. For each such cluster, we need cluster_id, size, stability (float number).\n- Build tree from parent mapping; note that clusters not retained (size < min_cluster_size) are dropped entirely, so the tree only includes retained clusters? The problem says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size. For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" Then \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So the selection process operates on retained clusters. The tree structure should be considered among retained clusters, but parent mapping may reference dropped clusters. We need to interpret the parent-child relationships only among retained clusters. When we drop a cluster, its children become as if they are children of the dropped cluster's parent? Or we treat the hierarchy as defined by parent mapping, but only consider relationships where both parent and child are retained? Typically in condensed tree, clusters that are too small are removed, and their children are attached to the nearest surviving ancestor. The candidate artifact says \"Condensed-tree construction\" and \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So we should reconstruct the hierarchy: For each retained cluster, its parent in the condensed tree is the nearest retained ancestor (could be null if root). The parent mapping provided is the original hierarchy. We need to find for each retained cluster, the next retained cluster up the chain (or null if none). Then we can determine descendant relationships for selection blocking.\n\nThe selection process: Sort retained clusters by stability descending, then cluster_id ascending (alphabetically?). The problem says \"by decreasing stability and cluster ID\" meaning sort by stability descending, ties broken by cluster_id (presumably ascending string order). Then iterate sorted list, select a cluster if no already selected descendant blocks it; when selecting, block its ancestors (prevent selection of ancestors that would include it? Actually \"block its ancestors\" means that once we select a cluster, its ancestors cannot be selected later because they would contain the selected cluster? Wait: The algorithm for selecting stable clusters is typically: sort by stability, then for each in order, if none of its descendants are already selected, select it and mark all its ancestors as blocked. This ensures that we never select a cluster that has a selected descendant (since descendant would be more stable? But stability order is descending, so descendant might be selected earlier? Actually we iterate from highest stability to lowest, so when we consider a cluster, any selected descendant would have higher stability and would have been considered earlier, so we would have blocked the ancestor already? Actually standard condensed tree selection: Rank clusters by stability descending. For each cluster, if it is not already blocked and none of its descendants are selected, then select it and block all ancestors. The blocking prevents selection of ancestors that would contain the selected cluster. So we need to maintain a set of blocked clusters (ancestors of selected ones). Also ensure we don't select a cluster that has a selected descendant (but since we process from high to low, descendant would have been processed later? Actually if descendant has lower stability, it would be processed later? Wait: Descendants have lower stability? Not necessarily; a child might have higher stability? Usually in HDBSCAN, children are more stable? In the example, need to examine. The candidate says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That means we check if any descendant (lower in tree) has been selected. Since we process from high stability to low, if a descendant has higher stability, it would have been processed earlier and selected (if not blocked). So when we later consider its ancestor, we must not select it because it would contain an already selected descendant. So the condition is: none of its descendants are already selected. And when we select, we block all ancestors (to prevent later selection of ancestors). But we also need to block the selected cluster itself? Typically we block ancestors only.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case: get min_cluster_size, clusters list, parent dict.\n2. Build original parent-child mapping: for each cluster_id, find its parent from parent dict. Note: parent dict may contain entries for all cluster_ids, and also for clusters not in clusters list? Unlikely. Build a dict child -> parent.\n3. Filter clusters: compute for each cluster stability and keep if size >= min_cluster_size.\n4. Build a mapping from cluster_id to its node info: size, stability.\n5. For retained clusters, need to find their condensed parent (nearest retained ancestor). We can do: for each retained cluster, traverse up the parent chain until we find a retained cluster or None. If we hit a non-retained parent, skip to its parent. Continue until root (parent is None). The first retained ancestor encountered (or None) becomes its condensed parent.\n6. Build condensed tree: for each retained cluster, record children list from parent-child mapping (condensed parent). Also we need to know descendants for each cluster. We can build tree structure recursively or compute descendant sets. Since selection requires checking if any descendant is selected, we can precompute all descendants (including indirect) for each retained cluster. Or during selection, we can use a data structure to check descendants efficiently. Since number of retained clusters is small (likely up to tens), we can compute descendants sets via DFS.\n7. Sort retained clusters by ( -stability, cluster_id ) ascending negative stability gives descending stability, then ascending cluster_id.\n8. Maintain selected set, blocked set (clusters that are ancestors of selected). Since we process in order, we can do:\n   - For each cluster in sorted order:\n        if cluster not in blocked and none of its descendants are in selected set:\n            select cluster (add to selected set)\n            block all its ancestors (add them to blocked set)\n   - Note: blocking ancestors should include immediate parent up to root, but we must be careful: we need to block all ancestors in the condensed tree (i.e., all clusters that are ancestors of the selected cluster). That includes the selected cluster's parent, grandparent, etc., but not the selected cluster itself (we don't need to block it because we already selected it, but it could be considered blocked? Not necessary). Also we must ensure that when we block ancestors, we don't accidentally block a cluster that later could be selected if it's not an ancestor? That's fine.\n9. After selection, produce output:\n   - \"clusters\": list of objects for all retained clusters (even those not selected) with cluster_id, size, stability (as float). Order? The output schema doesn't specify order, but probably as they appear in input or any order. I'll keep original order of retained clusters as they appear in input clusters list (filtering out small ones). Or sorted? The test may not care, but to be deterministic, I'll sort by cluster_id? Input example has c0, c1, c2. I'll keep original order.\n   - \"selected_cluster_ids\": list of selected cluster IDs in sorted order? The problem says \"sorted selected IDs\". It says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So selected_cluster_ids should be sorted. The sorting order? Probably by cluster_id ascending. Since we sorted selection by stability then cluster_id, the selected set order may not be sorted. So we need to sort the selected IDs alphabetically before output.\n\nEdge cases: \n- Some clusters may have no point_exit_lambdas? The schema shows it's always a list. Compute stability sum. If list empty, stability = 0.\n- birth_lambda and exit_lambdas could be negative? Not specified. Use max(0, exit - birth).\n- Floating point comparisons: stability is sum of floats, may have precision issues but fine.\n- Parent mapping: parent of root is null. Some clusters may not be in parent dict? Assume all clusters have entry.\n- If a cluster is not in parent dict, treat as root? But spec says parent mapping includes all. For robustness, treat missing as root.\n- Dropped clusters (size < min) may have children that are retained; we need to skip them in condensed parent chain.\n\nWe'll implement a function condensed_parent(child_id) that climbs up original parent chain until reaching a retained cluster or None.\n\nImplementation skeleton:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters = case['clusters']\n    parent_map = case['parent']\n    \n    # Build original cluster info dict\n    orig = {}\n    for c in clusters:\n        orig[c['cluster_id']] = c\n    \n    # Compute retained clusters\n    retained = {}\n    for c in clusters:\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\n        size = c['size']\n        if size >= min_size:\n            stability = sum(max(0, lam - c['birth_lambda']) for lam in c['point_exit_lambdas'])\n            retained[cid] = {'size': size, 'stability': stability, 'children': []}\n    \n    # Build condensed parent mapping for retained\n    condensed_parent = {}\n    for cid in retained:\n        # traverse original parent chain\n        cur = cid\n        while True:\n            p = parent_map.get(cur)\n            if p is None:\n                condensed_parent[cid] = None\n                break\n            if p in retained:\n                condensed_parent[cid] = p\n                break\n            cur = p\n        # If parent chain goes to non-retained and eventually root, condensed parent is None\n    \n    # Build tree from condensed parent\n    tree = {cid: {'parent': condensed_parent[cid], 'children': []} for cid in retained}\n    for cid in retained:\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\n        if p is not None:\n            tree[p]['children'].append(cid)\n    \n    # Compute descendants for each cluster (including self? For selection, we need to check if any descendant is selected. We'll compute all descendants recursively)\n    descendants = {}\n    def get_descendants(cid):\n        if cid in descendants:\n            return descendants[cid]\n        res = set()\n        for child in tree[cid]['children']:\n            res.add(child)\n            res.update(get_descendants(child))\n        descendants[cid] = res\n        return res\n    for cid in retained:\n        get_descendants(cid)\n    \n    # Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id)\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained.keys(), key=lambda cid: (-retained[cid]['stability'], cid))\n    \n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant is selected\n        desc = descendants[cid]\n        if desc & selected:\n            continue\n        # Select this cluster\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # Block all ancestors\n        # climb up condensed parent chain\n        cur = condensed_parent[cid]\n        while cur is not None:\n            blocked.add(cur)\n            cur = condensed_parent[cur]\n    \n    # Prepare output\n    # clusters list in original order of clusters in input (filter retained)\n    out_clusters = []\n    for c in clusters:\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\n        if cid in retained:\n            out_clusters.append({\n                'cluster_id': cid,\n                'size': retained[cid]['size'],\n                'stability': retained[cid]['stability']\n            })\n    selected_ids = sorted(selected)  # alphabetical\n    return {'clusters': out_clusters, 'selected_cluster_ids': selected_ids}\n\nTest with given fixtures: \nCase 000: min_cluster_size=3. clusters: c0 size7, c1 size4, c2 size2 (dropped). retained: c0, c1. \nCompute stabilities: \nc0: exit_lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9], birth=0.2 => (0.2,0.4,0.7) sum=1.3\nc1: exit [0.7,1.0,1.2], birth=0.5 => (0.2,0.5,0.7) sum=1.4\nParent mapping: c0 root, c1 parent c0, c2 parent c0 (but c2 dropped). So condensed parent: c1 parent c0 (since c0 retained). c0 parent None.\nTree: c0 children [c1], c1 children [].\nDescendants: c0: {c1}, c1: {}.\nSorted by stability: c1 (1.4) first, then c0 (1.3). \nProcess c1: not blocked, no descendants selected? descendant set empty => select c1. Block ancestors: parent c0 => block c0.\nProcess c0: c0 is blocked => skip.\nSelected = {c1}. Output clusters: c0 and c1 (in order original). selected_ids sorted: ['c0','c1']? No, selected is only c1, so ['c1']. So answer: clusters [c0 with stability1.3, c1 with stability1.4], selected ['c1'].\nCheck if that matches expected? Unknown, but algorithm seems correct.\n\nEdge: If multiple roots? Parent mapping may have multiple null entries. That's fine.\n\nReturn as JSON.\n\nNow produce the implementation code in Python. It must be a function solve(case) returning dict.\n\nWrap in output JSON with key \"implementation\".\n\nWe need to escape for JSON. Use json.dumps to encode the source string.\n\nWill produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1723,"completion_tokens":3745,"total_tokens":5468,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3046},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":59},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}